        -:  387:
        -:  388:/**
        -:  389: * For better understanding of the points that make a dimension:
        -:  390: *
        -:  391: * Note: historically KiCad called extension lines "feature lines", and also note that what we
        -:  392: * call the "crossbar line" here is more commonly called the "dimension line"
        -:  393: *
        -:  394: *              Start (feature point 1)         End (feature point 2)
        -:  395: *                |                              |
        -:  396: *                |   <-- extension lines -->    |
        -:  397: *                |                              |
        -:  398: *                |  m_arrowG2F      m_arrowD2F  |
        -:  399: *                | /                          \ |
        -:  400: * Crossbar start |/_______crossbar line________\| Crossbar end
        -:  401: *                |\           m_text           /|
        -:  402: *                | \                          / |
        -:  403: *                |  m_arrowG1F      m_arrowD1F  |
        -:  404: *                |                              |
        -:  405: *                m_featureLineGF  m_featureLineDF
        -:  406: */
        -:  407:
        -:  408:/**
        -:  409: * An aligned dimension measures the distance between two feature points.  It has a crossbar
        -:  410: * (dimension line) that stays parallel with the vector between the feature points.
        -:  411: *
        -:  412: * The height (distance from features to crossbar) can be set directly, or set by manipulating the
        -:  413: * crossbar start or end point (with the point editor).
        -:  414: */
        -:  415:class PCB_DIM_ALIGNED : public PCB_DIMENSION_BASE
        -:  416:{
